---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Home" bodyClass="home">
  <div class="home-layout">
    <aside class="home-sidebar">
      <img
        src="/images/profile.jpg"
        alt="Filippo Carmenati"
        class="profile-image"
        width="350"
        height="280"
      />
      <div class="sidebar-links">
        <Nav />
        <div class="social-icons">
          <a
            href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/filippo-carmenati-5215ba1b4/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            aria-label="LinkedIn"
          >
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true">
              <path d="M20.447 20.452h-3.554v-5.569c0-1.328-.027-3.037-1.852-3.037-1.853 0-2.136 1.446-2.136 2.939v5.667H9.351V9h3.414v1.561h.046c.477-.9 1.637-1.85 3.37-1.85 3.601 0 4.267 2.37 4.267 5.455v6.286zM5.337 7.433a2.062 2.062 0 1 1 0-4.124 2.062 2.062 0 0 1 0 4.124zM7.114 20.452H3.559V9h3.555v11.452z"/>
            </svg>
          </a>
          <a
            href="mailto:filippo.carmenati@gmail.com"
            aria-label="Email"
          >
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
              <rect x="3" y="5" width="18" height="14" rx="2"/>
              <path d="m3 7 9 6 9-6"/>
            </svg>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </aside>

    <div class="home-content">
      <p class="hello"><strong>Hi there! Were you looking for me?</strong></p>

      <p><strong>Benvenuto!</strong></p>

      <p>
        I am Filippo Carmenati, an <strong>agroecologist</strong> specializing in <strong>intercropping</strong>
        and interested in <strong>designing diversified cropping systems</strong> through a
        systems agroecology approach. My involvement in this field has taken
        me on an exciting journey, both intellectually and geographically.
      </p>

      <p>
        I first became interested in alternative cropping systems during a
        course I took while on Erasmus at
        <a
          href="https://www.nature.com/nature-index/institution-outputs/czech-republic/czech-university-of-life-sciences-prague-culs/54bf501d140ba0af038b4567"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >CULS</a> in 2017. Driven by curiosity,
        I went on to study sustainable agriculture, with a particular focus on
        <strong>intercropping</strong> systems. My research has ranged from the historical use
        of <strong>intercropping</strong> and crop rotations, which was the topic of my <strong>B.Sc.
        thesis</strong>, to variation in functional traits of cocoa under monoculture
        and agroforestry systems during my <strong>M.Sc. thesis</strong>. I later moved toward
        farm-scale experiments investigating underutilized crops and their
        integration into diversified cropping systems during my
        <strong><a
          href="https://www.research-collection.ethz.ch/entities/publication/dd909597-43d4-42df-827f-3807a08f9964"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >Ph.D.</a></strong> at
        Agroscope and ETH Zürich.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Diversity and interactions among system components have been
        the common thread connecting these experiences and continue to fuel
        my curiosity.</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        I have been fortunate to work with leading European research
        institutes and, above all, with wonderful people throughout my
        academic journey.
      </p>

      <p>
        While I am passionate about agroecology, I am equally interested in
        working across disciplines and with people from different
        backgrounds. My interests extend beyond agroecology to
        <strong>soil microbiology, computational science,</strong> and
        <strong>plant biology</strong>. On the other side of the coin, I am
        also curious <strong>about art, social sciences, and
        anthropology</strong>. I see these fields as offering complementary
        perspectives on interaction, diversity, and the relationships among
        components that can make a system richer and more resilient when
        thoughtfully designed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
